\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Official Rules for No Limit: A Bot Building Tournament Organized by Sequoia Capital Operations, LLC and Hosted by OpenAI and Citadel Securities\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
Sequoia Capital Operations, LLC (the "Organizer") organizes and administers the bot building competition described in these rules (the "Competition"). OpenAI OpCo, LLC ("OpenAI") and Citadel Securities are hosts (together, the "Hosts"). The Organizer is solely responsible for Competition administration, the competition platform, eligibility verification, scoring, winner selection, prize payment, tax reporting and withholding, and compliance with laws applicable to the events. The Hosts are not responsible for the Organizer's acts or omissions in performing those functions. By registering, each participant agrees to these rules as a binding agreement with the Organizer. The Hosts and their respective affiliates, officers, directors, employees, and agents are express third-party beneficiaries of the licenses, releases, indemnities, disclaimers, and limitations of liability that protect them under these rules. The Organizer, Hosts, and their respective affiliates, officers, directors, employees, and agents are collectively the "Competition Entities".\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1. ELIGIBILITY\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 1.1Participation is open to currently enrolled students at any university in the United States and Canada and students of U.S. and Canadian universities from the class of 2025 or 2026.\
1.2Participants must be 18 or older. Participants under 18 must have parental or guardian consent to compete in a regional tournament. If accepted, the Organizer will send a consent form for a parent or guardian to sign.\
1.3Gamemasters, judges, employees, officers, directors, and contractors of the Organizer, the Hosts, and their respective affiliates, and their immediate family members (spouse, parents, siblings, and children) and household members may not compete.\
1.4Teams may consist of one or two people. Each participant may compete in only one regional event. Teammates do not have to attend the same school.\
1.5Participation is free. Participants contribute nothing toward the prize pool. No purchase of OpenAI services or any other product or service is necessary to enter or win.\
1.6Participants must be legally eligible to participate and receive prizes under applicable law and must not be subject to sanctions or export restrictions that prohibit participation or receipt of a prize. Any use of OpenAI services must occur from a country or territory supported for the applicable service as set forth {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://platform.openai.com/docs/supported-countries"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 here}}. The Organizer may require proof of age, university affiliation, identity, and eligibility. Participants acting for an employer, university, or other entity must have all necessary permissions to participate, submit their code, grant the rights in these rules, and receive prizes. Void where prohibited by law.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 2. REGISTRATION AND SCHEDULE\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 2.1Each participant must register online by each deadline, including both members of a two-person team. Before completing registration, each participant must be given access to and affirmatively accept these rules, acknowledge the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pokerbot.sequoiacap.com/privacy.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 privacy notice}} identified in Section 8.2, and make any applicable photo and video election under Section 8.3.\
2.2Each regional will accept a limited number of teams. Places are offered on a rolling basis. All participants must register at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pokerbot.sequoiacap.com/index.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 pokerbot.sequoiacap.com}}\
2.3Participants must attend their regional in person for the full event. Check-in begins at 5:30 PM, and the regional event runs from 6:00-9:00 PM local time.\
2.4Events will take place on the following dates in 2026:\
- Friday, November 6 - Menlo Park, CA (deadline to register - Tuesday, November 3 at 11:59 PM PT)\
- Monday, November 9 - Cambridge, MA (deadline to register - Friday, November 6 at 11:59 PM ET)\
- Thursday, November 12 - Pittsburgh, PA (deadline to register - Monday, November 9 at 11:59 PM ET)\
- Sunday, November 15 - Finals in San Francisco, CA (Attendance is by invitation only, with invitations extended to the top-performing teams at each regional event.)\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 3. POKERBOT COMPETITION FORMAT\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 3.1These competition rules apply to both the regionals and the Finals.\
3.2The poker variant will be revealed on the day of each event at the start of the build phase. It will remain confidential until that announcement.\
3.3Teams have 60 minutes to build a Python bot that implements the provided Agent interface. Teams receive a starter kit containing an agent template, local simulator, Independent Chip Model (ICM) calculator, payout structure, and blind schedule.\
3.4Teams may use Astra and Codex to write, debug, and refine their bots during the build phase. Publicly available documentation is permitted.\
3.5Each team may submit one bot every five minutes. The team's most recently accepted submission becomes its active bot.\
3.6Submitted bots run in isolated sandboxes without network access. They cannot call OpenAI tools, external APIs, or any other outside service to make gameplay decisions.\
3.7Each bot must return a decision within one millisecond. The maximum file size is 100mb. If a bot encounters an error or exceeds the time limit, it automatically folds, or checks if checking is available.\
3.8Bot state resets at the start of each table. Opponents are anonymized so bots cannot identify specific teams.\
3.9Bots compete exclusively against other bots. No humans play in the bot competition. Chips and points exist only within the simulation and have no cash value.\
3.10No real money is bet by the bots. Participants, spectators, hosts, and all other attendees may not bet or wager on competition outcomes.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 4. SCORING\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 4.1There are six scored tournaments, labeled T1-T6. The first begins at the end of the 60-minute build phase, with subsequent tournaments starting 20 minutes apart.\
4.2Teams may continue editing and submitting bots after the build phase. At the start of each scored tournament, each team's active bot is frozen for that tournament. Any submissions accepted afterward apply to the next scored tournament.\
4.3Each scored tournament consists of 10,000 simulated eight-bot sit-and-gos. In each iteration, bots are randomly assigned to tables.\
4.4Each table plays until one bot holds all the chips or 200 hands have been played. Each bot starts with 2,000 chips. Blinds start at 10/20 and increase every 25 hands.\
4.5Points are awarded based on each bot's finishing position at its table. The point distribution may vary by poker variant and will be announced at the start of the build phase. An eliminated bot receives the points assigned to its finishing position. If a table reaches the 200-hand limit, the remaining points are allocated among the surviving bots using the Independent Chip Model (ICM).\
4.6If the number of competing bots is not divisible by eight, empty seats are filled with randomly selected copies of submitted bots. These filler bots earn no points.\
4.7A team's score for each scored tournament is its average points per table across the 10,000 iterations. Later tournaments carry greater weight: Final score = (T1 score \'d7 1) + (T2 score \'d7 2) + (T3 score \'d7 3) + (T4 score \'d7 4) + (T5 score \'d7 5) + (T6 score \'d7 6).\
4.8Ties are resolved by a tie-breaker challenge to be determined by the Organizer. The Organizer will announce the tie-breaker challenge and its objective skill-based scoring criteria.\
4.9Competition rankings and prizes depend solely on bot performance under this scoring system, subject to the tie-breaking rule.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 5. PRIZES AND ADVANCEMENT\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 5.1The top 5 teams at each regional receive $5,000 per team and advance to the Finals. We'll also select up to five additional wildcard teams across the regional games based on their scores.\
5.2The Finals follow the same competition format using a different poker variant. Finals prizes are:\
- First place: $100,000\
- Second place: $50,000\
- Third place: $25,000\
5.3Prizes are awarded per team and split equally between teammates. Winners must complete the required tax documentation. Prizes will be reported as required by law. All prizes will be distributed by Sequoia Capital Operations, LLC. Winners are responsible for all taxes on their prizes. Payments are subject to applicable withholding and receipt of all required eligibility certifications, tax forms, releases, and other prize acceptance documents. Hosts have no obligation to pay prizes or handle the Organizer's or winner's tax obligations.\
5.4Prizes will be paid within 30 days after the Organizer verifies the winner's eligibility and compliance with these rules and receives all required prize acceptance documents. Potential winners must return requested documents within 14 days after notification. Failure to do so, ineligibility, or a material rule violation may result in forfeiture, in which case the Organizer may select the next highest-ranked eligible team. Prizes and advancement remain subject to verification, including code review reasonably necessary to verify compliance.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 6. CONDUCT AND INTEGRITY\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 6.1Teams will be disqualified for colluding with other teams, copying another team's code, interfering with the platform, or using the submission endpoint in unintended ways. The Organizer may also disqualify participants for cheating, fraud, harassment, malicious code, security abuse, material misrepresentations, or other material violations of these rules, applicable law, or applicable service terms, including violations discovered after an event or an initial announcement of results.\
6.2The Organizer's technical lead has final authority over platform and timing decisions, including whether to delay a scored tournament. The events lead has final authority over rules and conduct decisions.\
6.3Participants must raise any dispute with a Gamemaster before the next scored tournament begins. Disputes concerning T6 must be raised within 5 minutes after its results are published. Hand logs are retained to support dispute resolution. These deadlines apply to requests for review of tournament results and do not limit the Organizer's ability to investigate misconduct, verify eligibility, correct errors, or enforce these rules after the deadlines.\
6.4Participants may not publicly share the poker variant or other unreleased event details before the event ends.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 7. USE OF AI TOOLS\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 7.1AI tools may assist teams with writing, debugging, and refining bot code as described in Section 3. They may not make live gameplay decisions for submitted bots.\
7.2The Organizer may use an AI model after hands have been played to select or rank hands for replay on spectator screens. This use does not affect gameplay, scoring, rankings, or prizes.\
7.3Using OpenAI tools is optional at every stage of the tournament.\
7.4Use of OpenAI services is subject to the applicable OpenAI agreement and Usage Policies, available at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openai.com/policies/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 https://openai.com/policies/}}, and any applicable event-specific access terms. These rules do not amend those terms or authorize any otherwise prohibited use. Participants must review and test AI-generated code and remain responsible for their submissions. OpenAI does not guarantee that its services will be continuously available, that outputs will be accurate or error-free, or that using its services will produce any particular Competition result. Nothing in these rules grants rights in OpenAI services beyond those granted under the applicable OpenAI agreement.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

